  "summary": "Pinal County Sheriff Ross Teeple testified before a U.S. Senate Judiciary subcommittee hearing on Flock's ALPR network, calling for federal oversight after his county ended its Flock contract in August. Other witnesses including a wrongfully jailed Florida woman, an ACLU counsel, and a security researcher raised concerns about misuse, vulnerabilities, and AI capabilities, while senators from both parties called for guardrails. The article also notes Cottonwood and roughly a dozen other Arizona cities canceling or reviewing Flock contracts.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Let's not allow a private company that is way overreaching in their artificial intelligence technology to strip us of a valuable law enforcement tool",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "A Flock camera captured my vehicle a few miles from a terrible crash I had nothing to do with that led to 13 days in jail",
      "paragraph_idx": 30
    },
    {
      "quote": "The time has come for Congress to ban any law enforcement entity who receives federal funding from using ALPRs",
      "paragraph_idx": 34
    },
    {
      "quote": "Cottonwood became the latest city in Arizona to announce it is canceling its Flock contract",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    }
